db/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436025 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "adf8aa6a-75ee-5eec-b556-6b1e3d610f4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={y}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5ccf563-8a57-5f1f-ad06-ef72dc726ab7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436028 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equations $y=\\pm \\sqrt{c}$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [0,\\infty ),$ the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c44ae1b59af6ac6f64efedcf4242aaad07ddcf98c2b63c4aeae7958a6622874.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
